# tenant-service-aliases.yaml.template
#
# The DNS aliases that make a Developer Team Agent's rendered config resolvable (03 §3; Phase 3),
# rendered by `provision_12_deploy_agent_tiers.sh` and applied by it BEFORE the Agent CR. THIS FILE
# IS THE SOURCE. Two copies are DERIVED from it and must never be hand-edited: the committed
# exemplar under examples/gitops-repo/ (checked byte-for-byte by
# local-dev/tests/reference-render.py) and the matching propose-developer-team skill asset with the
# tenant namespace parameterised (checked by local-dev/test_skill_templates.py). Edit this template,
# then regenerate both.
#
# WHY THESE EXIST AT ALL. The controller renders every agent's config with the shared services
# hard-coded to the POD'S OWN namespace (agent_manifests.go):
#     model.baseURL    = http://litellm.<ns>.svc.cluster.local/v1
#     GITHUB_TOKEN_URL = http://github-token-minter.<ns>.svc.cluster.local:8080/token
# For a dev-team pod the A1 placement clause forces metadata.namespace == the tenant namespace, so
# those resolve to `litellm.${TENANT_NAMESPACE}.svc` and
# `github-token-minter.${TENANT_NAMESPACE}.svc` — names that do not exist. The real services live
# once per cluster in ${CONTROL_NAMESPACE}, alongside the cluster-admin agent, which uses them
# directly and needs no alias. These ExternalName Services CNAME the in-namespace names to the
# shared ones, so the rendered config works unmodified without teaching the controller
# per-namespace service topology.
#
# WHY THE INSTALL PATH APPLIES THIS, AND WHY BEFORE THE POD. Until Phase 8 the aliases existed ONLY
# as a committed exemplar and a skill template — nothing applied them, so a real multi-tier install
# produced a dev-team agent whose every model call resolved to NXDOMAIN. That is the same defect
# class as the egress policies and the tenant quota (LSN-006/LSN-007): a manifest that documents a
# requirement is not a manifest that satisfies it. Applying them before the Agent CR means the pod's
# first inference call resolves rather than failing while `wait_for_k8s_resource ... Available`
# counts down against a DNS error that never mentions DNS.
#
# ExternalName is pure DNS: no ClusterIP, no proxying, and NO PORT REMAPPING. The client connects to
# whatever port the rendered URL specifies (80 for litellm, 8080 for the minter), and that port must
# exist on the target Service in ${CONTROL_NAMESPACE} — changing a shared Service's port silently
# breaks every tenant, because nothing here would need to change to stay wrong. The developer-team
# egress NetworkPolicy admits the resulting hop (netpol-agent-egress.yaml.template, rule 2); the
# alias resolves the name, the policy permits the packet, and BOTH are required.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: litellm
  namespace: ${TENANT_NAMESPACE}
  labels:
    kube-agents/tenant: ${TENANT_NAMESPACE}
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: gitops
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: litellm.${CONTROL_NAMESPACE}.svc.cluster.local
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: github-token-minter
  namespace: ${TENANT_NAMESPACE}
  labels:
    kube-agents/tenant: ${TENANT_NAMESPACE}
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: gitops
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: github-token-minter.${CONTROL_NAMESPACE}.svc.cluster.local
